WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.275
Hi, I'm Vesna!

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:05.887
A viral chart says full attention has vanished from open models.

00:00:05.887 --> 00:00:06.872
I recounted it.

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.747
August to November: zero percent used full attention throughout.

00:00:13.127 --> 00:00:16.636
The post names no source, but the watermark does.

00:00:16.636 --> 00:00:20.925
It is Sebastian Raschka's gallery, a hundred and twenty-two hand-picked architectures.

00:00:22.242 --> 00:00:23.995
So I read every config.

00:00:23.995 --> 00:00:27.149
On the gallery's own list, the chart reproduces exactly.

00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:29.953
The last bar has no grey: nothing all-full.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.805
Widen the list to a hundred and sixty-seven releases, and the zero goes.

00:00:34.805 --> 00:00:38.589
Fourteen point three percent still use full attention in every layer.

00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:41.929
Filter by size and you see where it went.

00:00:41.929 --> 00:00:44.231
Under forty billion parameters, four of eleven.

00:00:44.231 --> 00:00:46.533
Over three hundred billion, one of eighteen.

00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:48.920
Thirty-five releases.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.342
Five all full, nineteen hybrids, eleven with no full layer at all.

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:55.342
But ask a different question.

00:00:55.342 --> 00:00:59.133
Which of them can still read every earlier token, in some layer?

00:00:59.133 --> 00:00:59.765
All thirty-five.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:03.644
Here is how.

00:01:03.644 --> 00:01:04.902
One cell per layer.

00:01:04.902 --> 00:01:07.418
K2 Horizon: full attention all the way down.

00:01:07.838 --> 00:01:10.591
Gemma four: five window layers, then one full one.

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:14.597
Qwen: three layers that fold the past into a fixed state,

00:01:14.597 --> 00:01:15.673
then one full one.

00:01:16.093 --> 00:01:17.816
Kimi K three, the same rhythm.

00:01:19.893 --> 00:01:23.510
Then K three point one Pro put an indexer in front of

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:24.716
those twenty-four full layers.

00:01:24.716 --> 00:01:25.922
They are still there.

00:01:25.922 --> 00:01:26.826
They stopped counting.

00:01:27.246 --> 00:01:29.777
MiniMax did the same to its last three.

00:01:29.777 --> 00:01:31.043
Same backbone, new class.

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.003
Those were drawings.

00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:36.914
These are the real strips, from the article's widget.

00:01:36.914 --> 00:01:40.148
Red is full attention, purple is sparse with an indexer.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.534
Why do it?

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:42.895
Memory.

00:01:42.895 --> 00:01:46.871
Qwen at its full context keeps about seventeen gigabytes of cache.

00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:49.039
With every layer full, nearly sixty-nine.

00:01:49.419 --> 00:01:51.561
Sparse attention is a different bargain.

00:01:51.561 --> 00:01:55.846
At a million tokens MiniMax keeps a hundred and forty-five gigabytes, more

00:01:55.846 --> 00:01:57.274
than plain attention would.

00:01:57.274 --> 00:02:00.130
What it saves is reads: sixteen point four.

00:02:01.725 --> 00:02:03.799
Open models are not dropping global attention.

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.057
They are putting an indexer, a window or a fixed state

00:02:07.057 --> 00:02:08.242
in front of it.

00:02:10.775 --> 00:02:12.644
The chart is real, for its list.

00:02:12.644 --> 00:02:13.712
The zero is not.

00:02:13.712 --> 00:02:14.514
Thanks for watching!

00:02:15.375 --> 00:02:17.476
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:17.476 --> 00:02:18.076
I'm Vesna.

00:02:18.076 --> 00:02:18.376
Bye!
